{
  "id": "PSI-20",
  "summary_plain": "This investigation uses a burning rate emulator to study how flames behave on condensed-phase materials in quiet microgravity conditions aboard the International Space Station. Researchers burn gaseous fuel mixtures on porous burners to simulate the burning rates of solid plastics like PMMA and nylon. Understanding these quiescent microgravity flames is critical for improving spacecraft fire safety and material screening.",
  "summary_expert": "The ACME Burning Rate Emulator (BRE) investigation examines quiescent microgravity burning of CH4/N2 and C2H4/N2 mixtures on circular porous burners with diameters of 25 and 50 mm. Tests were planned across varied chamber pressures, diluents, and oxygen concentrations, including spacecraft exploration atmospheres of 14.7 psia with 21 percent oxygen, 10.2 psia with 26.5 percent oxygen, and 8.2 psia with 34 percent oxygen. Results showed that quasi-steady burning corresponding to condensed-phase materials like PMMA and Nylon can occur in microgravity without forced air flow, particularly in elevated-oxygen environments, whereas flames in normal 21 percent oxygen atmospheres self-extinguished quickly.",
  "key_findings": [
    {
      "text": "Quasi-steady burning corresponding to various condensed-phase materials such as PMMA and Nylon was observed in microgravity despite the absence of air flow.",
      "source": {
        "psi_id": "PSI-20",
        "doi": "10.60555/vb93-3y72",
        "file": "metadata/PSI-20.json#researchImpacts",
        "page": null
      },
      "provenance": "Google Gemini (free tier)"
    },
    {
      "text": "Ongoing combustion without air flow was notably observed in elevated-oxygen atmospheres, whereas such flames in a normal atmosphere of 21% oxygen self-extinguished in a short time.",
      "source": {
        "psi_id": "PSI-20",
        "doi": "10.60555/vb93-3y72",
        "file": "metadata/PSI-20.json#researchImpacts",
        "page": null
      },
      "provenance": "Google Gemini (free tier)"
    },
    {
      "text": "Generally, it has been found that nominal oxygen mole fractions of 26 % and above can lead to steady flames which are thin blue in color and grow slowly over time for minutes.",
      "source": {
        "psi_id": "PSI-20",
        "doi": "10.60555/vb93-3y72",
        "file": "documents/PSI-20/PSI-20_Reports_Dissertation-PD-revised_.pdf",
        "page": 140
      },
      "provenance": "quoted from PSI document (rules, no LLM)"
    }
